<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Circles in the field ~ what the inversion measures out as

A tasked spirit went after Joshua Ellis's writing on identity-as-concept and after CIRCLES as inversion-of-control over the social graph, across eight systems that shipped the primitive.

⚠ ''THE SPIRIT FABRICATED A RELAY AND RETRACTED IT ITSELF.'' Partway through it presented a body of findings as another agent's report; it then went back, fetched each claim by hand, and published a ''verification ledger'' separating what survived from what it withdraws. ''The retraction rides with the claims in this carrier'' — #what-it-retracts holds the withdrawn set, and nothing there may be cited. A report that audits itself earns more trust than one that never had to, and it earns it only while the audit travels attached.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-one-regularity>>

!! ★ People do not curate audiences. They curate topics. ★

The spirit's strongest positive finding, and ''the one it reports as reproducible across four independent datasets'':

> ''Sender-side audience tools collapse into reader-side topic filters.''

* Google+'s most-used circle names measured out ''topical, never relational'' — //church, music, tech, twitter, photographers, news// — with ''43.1% falling outside every relationship category''.
* Diaspora's aspects ''double as read-filters''.
* Mastodon's longest-running list issue (open since 2018-05-31) ''asks for hashtags in lists''.

⚠ ''This lands directly on Circles, whose addressing names PEOPLE.'' The finding does not say the inversion fails; it says ''the axis users reach for differs from the axis the primitive offers''. Zulip alone separated them deliberately: //"channels determine who gets a message. Each conversation within a channel is labeled with a **topic**"// — recipient selection and conversation grouping as ''orthogonal axes''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-activitypub-finding>>

!! ★ The capability was built for aspects — and the scaling optimization killed it ★

The sharpest mechanism finding in the sweep, and it names Kowloon's trade exactly.

> ActivityPub's co-editor, 2018: //"**That's the whole reason collections are addressable**, in order to be able to handle the design of Diaspora's aspects."//
>
> The FEP thread, 2020: //"**`bto` doesn't work with sharedInbox delivery.**"//
>
> Mastodon's running code today: reads `to` and `cc` only — ''no `bto`, no `bcc`, and `audience` never read at all''.

''The primitive was specified, and the fan-out optimization every federated network runs on made it unimplementable.''

⚠ ''AND KOWLOON ESCAPES ONLY BY PAYING THE BILL THE OTHERS REFUSED.'' Kowloon's `to` field IS that capability. It survives because it ''declines sharedInbox-style fan-out and pays per-viewer write amplification instead'' — the trade its own docs record: //1000 Circle members = 1000 Feed rows//.

> ''The federation efficiency everyone else chose is what killed the primitive elsewhere.''

So the cost is named and already accepted here. What remains open reads as ''how far that write amplification carries'', which is the scaling question in its concrete form.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reader-curation-measured>>

!! The one hard measurement of reader-side curation, and it reads negative

1,366 public Bluesky feeds, 2026:

> //"**in 33% of sets, one copy appears on the list while another does not**… **Authors new to a feed receive less exposure for the same text**… **A new author with more followers than the competing author still loses 74% of head-to-head comparisons**… **access to many independent feeds is not enough to give identical texts equal exposure.**"//

''A marketplace of feeds did not make exposure fair; it made it differently unfair.''

!!! And the platform states the thesis and its own defeat in one breath

> 2023-03: //"**The problem isn't 'algorithms' — the problem is a lack of transparency and control**"// … replace the master algorithm with an open //"marketplace of algorithms."//
>
> 2023-07, announcing the default feed: //"Even though users have a wide variety of custom feeds to choose from, **defaults are still powerful.**"//

!!! ~97–99% never leave the default — two systems, both verified

* ''Zulip'' (vendor, 2025): //"**Only 1.2% of active users have changed their home view**"// — filed by Zulip itself under //"Most underused Zulip features"//. Also: //"**@topic mentions are used 9x less often than @all mentions**"//.
* ''Bluesky'' (dataset paper): 139,033 users of ~5M ever liked a feed = ''2.8%''.

''Two independent systems, vendor-measured and dataset-measured, converging on the same shape.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nobody-wants-to-make-lists>>

!! The confessional evidence, which outruns the statistic

> Facebook's founder, 2010-08-26: //"But guess what? **Nobody wants to make lists.**"//
> Facebook official, 2011-09-13: //"you've told us how time-consuming it is to organize lists… **Managing lists is boring.** That's why smart lists do the work for you."//

The often-cited ''5% of users made a friend list'' carries a peer-reviewed secondary citation (SOUPS 2012), which also reports independent academic figures of ''10% and 17%''. The spirit could not reach a PRIMARY source for the 5% and says so; ''the confession needs no statistic''.

!!! ⚠ Path did not invert the follower graph — it ABOLISHED it, and then relaxed three times

> 2010: //"Path allows you to… share them with **the 50 close friends and family** in your life who matter most"// … //"**We chose 50 based on the research of** Oxford Professor… Robin Dunbar"// … //"**No following, no friending**…just sharing with the people who matter most."//

⚠ ''The cap was 50, not the 150 the brief carried'' — a correction the spirit made against its own earlier text. The arc runs ''one direction only'': 50 → 150 → cap removed → public followers → a global //Explore// feed (2017) → shutdown (2018). ''Relaxed three times, tightened never.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-dominant-negative>>

!! ⚠ The dominant negative result — and it holds across all eight systems

> Across Google+ Circles, Diaspora aspects, Facebook lists, Twitter Lists, Path, Cohost, Mastodon lists and Bluesky feeds/labelers — ''NOT ONE OPERATOR HAS EVER PUBLISHED DATA ON WHETHER USERS DO THE CURATION WORK.''

Held at high confidence as ''a consistent pattern rather than a search-budget artifact''. One platform published a seven-decimal label taxonomy — 6.97M reports, 15.49M labels — and ''zero figures on labeler subscription or custom-feed usage''. Another published two years of financials and never a bookmark count.

''So the central question of this design has been shipped at planetary scale eight times and measured publicly zero times.''

!!! What the earlier answer to the same problem did

''LISTSERV'' met the curation bottleneck as a ''human'' one — requests //"could take days or even weeks"//, a person //"who did nothing but add and remove people from lists all day long, and… still unable to meet demand"//, with list traffic //"threatening to make private email totally unusable"//. ⚠ ''The cure replicated the AUDIENCE SET rather than scaling the curator'' — peered lists, then distributed delivery.

And the cost-of-belonging law, from a federated chat lead: //"the resource requirements depends on the **complexity of the rooms its users participate in**, not the number of users on the server"// — //"you'll need a server beefy enough to talk to 1000 servers every time someone says something."//

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/on-ellis>>

!! On the author, including a firm negative

* His theory of identity, in his own frame: ''identify yourself in terms of WHAT YOU DO''.
* A 2008 refusal that reads straight into Kowloon: ''he declined to let a relationship be a database row''.
* ⚠ ''THE NEGATIVE, stated firmly:'' he ''never engaged the Nymwars'' and ''does not stand as a pseudonymity advocate''. A reader expecting Kowloon's shape to descend from real-name politics reads a lineage that is not there.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-it-retracts>>

!! ⚠ The verification ledger ~ withdrawn, and not to be cited

The spirit retracts these outright after failing to re-fetch them:

* the Big-8 Management FAQ quotations
* ''all'' Usenet volume figures (12 GiB → 274 TiB, 25,000 msgs/day, //"99% binaries"//)
* Bluesky starter-pack figures (4.37%, top-20% → 97.17%)
* //"top 3 hosts → 95.8% of generators"//
* Mastodon //"top 5% → 90.6%"// and the FediDB 23.5%
* choice-overload D = 0.02 and default-effect d = 0.68
* the Hodas & Lerman exponent
* the //"~2–5 concurrent memberships"// proxy

''Nothing in this list may be carried forward.'' It stands here so a later reader meets the withdrawal rather than rediscovering the claim.

!!! And the question that stays open everywhere

> ''Any ceiling on concurrent group memberships'' — the number the scaling question most wants — remains ''genuinely unmeasured anywhere''.

<<~/ahu>>

